 "raw_reply": "{\n  \"experiment_id\": \"EXP-044\",\n  \"observed_evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The selection rule ranks by mean ROC-AUC minus 0.25×fold std, then runtime, using training CV only.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-044 / evidence.selection_rule\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"extra_trees has mean ROC-AUC 0.7366 and std 0.00718 across 3 folds (1 repeat), with default parameters (model_params is empty).\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-044 / evidence.model_results[model=extra_trees].metrics.roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"logistic_regression has a slightly higher mean ROC-AUC (0.7376) and a larger std (0.01718).\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-044 / evidence.model_results[model=logistic_regression].metrics.roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Per-fold ROC-AUC (fold 1, 2, 3): logistic_regression 0.7395, 0.7196, 0.7538; extra_trees 0.7328, 0.7322, 0.7449. logistic_regression is higher in folds 1 and 3, extra_trees in fold 2.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-044 / evidence.model_results[model=logistic_regression].fold_metrics[*].roc_auc; evidence.model_results[model=extra_trees].fold_metrics[*].roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The 95% CIs for ROC-AUC overlap heavily: extra_trees [0.7285, 0.7448] and logistic_regression [0.7182, 0.7571].\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-044 / evidence.model_results[model=extra_trees].metrics.roc_auc.ci95_low/ci95_high; evidence.model_results[model=logistic_regression].metrics.roc_auc.ci95_low/ci95_high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"logistic_regression has lower mean log_loss (0.3809 vs 0.3844), lower mean Brier (0.1191 vs 0.1203) and lower mean ECE-10 (0.0205 vs 0.0267) than extra_trees. extra_trees has slightly higher mean average precision (0.3233 vs 0.3198).\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-044 / evidence.model_results[model=logistic_regression|extra_trees].metrics.{log_loss,brier,ece_10,avg_precision}.mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Fold-level recall at threshold 0.5 is very low for both models. Mean recall is 0.0828 for extra_trees and 0.0162 for logistic_regression.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-044 / evidence.model_results[model=extra_trees|logistic_regression].metrics.recall.mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"hist_gradient_boosting, lightgbm and xgboost have lower mean ROC-AUC (0.7200, 0.7156, 0.7092). hist_gradient_boosting reports no feature_stability entries.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-044 / evidence.model_results[model=hist_gradient_boosting|lightgbm|xgboost].metrics.roc_auc.mean; evidence.model_results[model=hist_gradient_boosting].feature_stability\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Every model was evaluated with 3 folds, 1 repeat and 16 features in the 'raw' stage, using baseline optimization with no tuning.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-044 / evidence.model_results[*].{folds,repeats,feature_count_mean,stage,optimization}\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The bundle's limitations list is empty, although claim EXP-044-C1 declares a limitation (no locked holdout yet).\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-044 / limitations; claims[EXP-044-C1].limitations\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"interpretation\": \"Inference: applying the stated rule to the cited means and stds gives scores of about 0.7349 for extra_trees and 0.7333 for logistic_regression (my arithmetic, not a reported field). extra_trees therefore leads by about 0.0015, entirely because of the lower fold std. Its raw mean ROC-AUC is not higher than logistic_regression's, and the two sets of fold scores overlap heavily. With 3 folds and 1 repeat, the std estimates are too noisy for 'stable' to separate these two models. Nothing in the evidence shows extra_trees beating a regularized linear baseline, and logistic_regression is better on log_loss, Brier and ECE. Boosted models trail on ROC-AUC under default settings, but they were not tuned, so no algorithm-family ranking follows. Feature importances are a mix of coefficient-based (logistic_regression) and impurity-based (trees) measures and do not support causal or decision-relevant claims.\",\n  \"decision\": \"Retain extra_trees only as a provisional training-CV candidate, tied with logistic_regression. Do not describe it as the strongest or most stable family, and do not approve it for deployment or holdout promotion until the paired repeated-CV comparison below is complete. No feature is approved.\",\n  \"risks_and_missing_evidence\": [\n    \"Only 3 folds with 1 repeat: the std and the 0.25×std penalty are unreliable, and a 0.0015 rule-score margin is well inside fold noise.\",\n    \"The CV split is not described. Stratification, grouping by user or session, and time ordering are all unspecified. online_shoppers sessions can share returning visitors, may contain duplicate rows, and cover a limited set of months, so random folds could inflate scores or distort the ranking. Month is a feature, which adds a seasonality risk.\",\n    \"No decision-time or production-availability review of the features. ExitRates, BounceRates, the page-count and duration aggregates (Administrative, Informational, ProductRelated and their durations) and SpecialDay may be computed over the whole session, including behavior after the decision point. Their availability when a prediction would be made is unverified.\",\n    \"It is not documented why the 16-feature raw set was chosen, or whether any columns (e.g. PageValues) were dropped for leakage reasons. The claim says 'raw features' without a preprocessing or encoding description, and the tree and linear models may be treated differently (scaling, categorical encoding, imputation).\",\n    \"All models use default hyperparameters, so the family comparison mixes algorithm differences with untuned-default effects.\",\n    \"The selection rule uses ROC-AUC only, with an arbitrary 0.25 std penalty. The runtime tiebreak is not applied and would favor the faster logistic_regression. The secondary metrics (log_loss, Brier, ECE) favor logistic_regression.\",\n    \"Threshold-dependent metrics at 0.5 (recall, F1) are close to degenerate for the minority class. The class prevalence and any cost context are not in the evidence, so these metrics should not support any operating-point claim.\",\n    \"Feature-importance stability is not comparable across models (logistic_regression importance is presumably coefficient-scale-based, trees are impurity-based). hist_gradient_boosting has none. Top-10 frequencies from 3 folds carry little weight, and importance is not evidence of causal effect.\",\n    \"The locked holdout has not been used. This is correct for selection, but it means the result is a training-CV estimate only.\"\n  ],\n  \"challenges_to_claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-044-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The claim names extra_trees the leader on a rule-score margin of about 0.0015 over logistic_regression, which has the higher raw mean ROC-AUC (0.7376 vs 0.7366). The fold scores and CIs overlap, and logistic_regression wins 2 of 3 folds. The lead comes from a noisy 3-fold std penalty, so it is not distinguishable from a tie.\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-044-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The '±0.0072' in the statement is not labeled as a std (it matches the 3-fold sample std). Presenting it as a stability margin overstates precision at n=3, and the declared limitations omit the small fold count and 1 repeat.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-044-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The question asks about the 'algorithm family', but only default-parameter, untuned, single-run models were compared. The result cannot be read as a family-level ranking.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-044-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The claim does not address validation leakage (grouping, duplicates, time) or decision-time feature availability. Session-aggregate features 